            <ArticleTitle>Elbow injuries in the throwing athlete.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>571-85</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ocl.2014.06.012</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0030-5898(14)00095-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>High valgus and extension loads imparted to the athlete's elbow during repetitive overhead throwing can lead to acute and chronic pathology. Over time, normal soft tissue and bony stabilizing structures of the elbow undergo progressive structural changes and can succumb to injury. Modern diagnostic modalities, including plain radiographs, computed tomography, and magnetic resonance imaging, in addition to arthroscopy, can aid in diagnosis. Although nonoperative management is often successful, surgical intervention may be necessary before allowing return to play.</AbstractText>
